---
// Documents the per-dataset private-holdout split (20–40% private) enforced
// by `synthbench.private_holdout` and surfaced in the leaderboard as the
// "verified" / "flagged" verification badge. This is the one place the
// holdout is explained; the anti-gaming section links here.
import MethodSection from "./MethodSection.astro";
---

<MethodSection id="private-holdout" title="Private holdout split" fold>
  <p class="sb-prose sb-measure" slot="lede">
    Every holdout-enabled dataset is deterministically split into a public subset and a private subset. The
    private fraction depends on dataset size, chosen to preserve statistical power: 20% for large datasets
    (OpinionsQA, WVS, GSS), 30% for medium ones (SubPOP, GlobalOpinionQA), and 40% for small ones
    (Michigan, NTIA, Eurobarometer).
  </p>

  <div class="sb-prose">
    <p>
      Per-question human distributions for the private subset are removed from every public SynthBench
      artifact (the site, the run-detail JSON, and the question-explorer pages), so a submitter can't recover
      the private answer key from our published files.
    </p>
    <p>
      Submissions must include results for every question, public and private. We (the SynthBench
      maintainers) score the private subset locally against the withheld answer key and publish the
      resulting <code class="sb-mcode">sps_private</code> alongside <code class="sb-mcode">sps_public</code>.
      Rows whose public/private SPS delta is within the review threshold get a
      <span class="sb-tag sb-ok">✓ verified</span> badge. Rows outside the tolerance are
      <span class="sb-tag sb-warn">⚠ flagged</span> for review.
    </p>
    <p>
      The partition is derived from an unsalted SHA-256 hash of
      <code class="sb-mcode">dataset_name + ":" + question_key</code>. That makes it stable across runs,
      machines, and Python versions without needing a seed file. It also means the list of private questions
      is not secret. The hash is public code, run artifacts carry explicit
      <code class="sb-mcode">is_holdout</code> flags, and anyone can compute any question's membership. This
      is deliberate and follows the Kaggle model: what is withheld is the private subset's answer key (the
      per-question human distributions), not the list of held-out questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="sb-ink">Limits.</strong> The source surveys behind these datasets are publicly
      downloadable upstream, and our open-source adapters will rebuild the full per-question distributions,
      private subset included, for anyone who runs them. A determined actor can therefore reconstruct the
      private targets. The holdout is a fabrication detector and a deterrent against casual overfitting. It
      does not provide cryptographic secrecy. It raises the effort from "copy our published JSON" to
      "reprocess the upstream source". A holdout that models cannot memorize would need newly fielded human
      data on a rotating schedule, which is a separate data-acquisition program.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="sb-ink">Why have a holdout.</strong> There are two separate reasons. First, future LLMs
      may train on SynthBench itself, and holding out 20–40% per dataset slows that loop. Second, and more
      important since per-question data was made public: once per-question human distributions are visible,
      a bad actor could fabricate a submission JSON that matches them exactly. The private subset is a cheap
      way to catch this. A fabricator has no signal on the hidden keys, so their public and private SPS
      diverge sharply.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div id="verification-badges" class="sb-prose">
    <h3 class="sb-h3 sb-mh3">Reading the verified / flagged badges</h3>
    <p>
      Every leaderboard row on a holdout-enabled dataset carries one of two badges, computed at publish time
      by comparing the row's SPS on the public subset against its SPS on the private subset
      (<code class="sb-mcode">|sps_public − sps_private|</code>, the published
      <code class="sb-mcode">sps_public_private_delta</code>):
    </p>
    <ul class="sb-mlist sb-mlist-plain">
      <li>
        <span class="sb-tag sb-ok">✓ verified</span>: the public and private scores are within the review
        threshold. This is what an honest run looks like.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="sb-tag sb-warn">⚠ flagged</span>: the public/private divergence exceeded the review
        threshold, so the row is held for review.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong class="sb-ink">What a flag means.</strong> A flag comes from a statistical anomaly detector and
      is not proof of cheating. A large divergence is what fabrication looks like (a fabricator can match the
      public answer key but has no signal on the hidden one). It can also come from overfitting to the public
      subset or, on datasets with a small private subset, from ordinary sampling noise concentrated in a few
      questions. Treat a flagged score with caution, and rely on the row's
      <code class="sb-mcode">sps_private</code> when deciding how much to trust it. A flag is not an
      accusation against the submitter.
    </p>
    <p>
      The threshold is 0.05 on runs with hundreds of questions, where honest deltas are under 0.02. A run with
      fewer private questions gets a wider threshold, 0.5 divided by the square root of the smaller subset's
      question count. A 100-question run has 20 to 30 private questions, so its threshold is about 0.1.
      Fabricated submissions show deltas of 0.2 or more and are flagged either way. Both scores, the delta,
      and the threshold are published on each row, so you can apply a stricter or looser standard yourself.
    </p>
  </div>
</MethodSection>
